## Data Fetching

The data of the app SHOULD be fetched at the page level and passed down to child
components.

The child components SHOULD NOT make API calls or fetch data themselves, but
instead define and use `emits` to communicate changes back to the parent page.

## Good example

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
const { $trpc } = useNuxtApp();
const { data, refresh } = await useAsyncData(
  "users",
  async () => await $trpc.users.list.query(),
);
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    <p>{{ data?.length }} users</p>
    <TableAdminUsers
      :items="data || []"
      @delete="
        async (id) => {
          await $trpc.users.delete.mutate({ id });
          refresh();
        }
      "
    />
  </div>
</template>
```

## Bad example

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
const { $trpc } = useNuxtApp();
// Fetching directly without useAsyncData or useQuery might not work well with SSR
const data = await $trpc.users.list.query();
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    <p>{{ data?.length }} users</p>
    <!-- Call the API to delete the user -->
    <TableAdminUsers :items="data || []" />
  </div>
</template>
```

## Nested Pages

If you are using nested pages. Eg:

```bash
tree app/pages/
app/pages/
├── demo
│   ├── page-a.vue
│   ├── page-b.vue
│   └── page-c.vue
└── demo.vue
```

You can render the pages with the URLs:

```bash
http://localhost:3000/demo/page-a
http://localhost:3000/demo/page-b
http://localhost:3000/demo/page-c
```

The `demo.vue` page will render the subpages in the `<NuxtPage />` component:

- `page-a.vue`
- `page-b.vue`
- `page-c.vue`

Sample of working `demo.vue` page:

```vue
<!-- app/pages/demo.vue -->
<template>
  <div>
    <h1>Demo</h1>
    <NuxtPage />
  </div>
</template>
```

### Bad example

The following does not work:

```bash
tree app/pages/
app/pages/
├── demo
│   ├── index.vue
│   ├── page-a.vue
│   ├── page-b.vue
│   └── page-c.vue
└── demo.vue
```

Neither does this:

```bash
tree app/pages/
app/pages/
├── demo
│   ├── index.vue
│   ├── page-a.vue
│   ├── page-b.vue
└── └── page-c.vue
```
